# Sequence Diagram — Sync Worker → Google Sheets
# Paste into https://play.d2lang.com/
#
# REV 4 fix: every note now has a UNIQUE key. Reusing `w.note` declared one
#            object five times, which D2 stretched across the whole diagram as
#            a grey band. Group-wrapped `alt` blocks also double-shaded; the
#            alt blocks are now top level.

shape: sequence_diagram

q: Redis Queue
w: Sync Worker
db: PostgreSQL
sa: GCP Service Account
gs: Google Sheets API v4
raw: "_raw tab (append-only)"
view: "Tickets tab (QUERY view)"
ops: Ops Alert

q -> w: "SyncTicketJob"

claim: Claim a batch of pending work {
  w -> db: "SELECT * FROM sync_outbox\nWHERE status = 'PENDING'\nORDER BY created_at\nLIMIT 100 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED"
  db -> w: "up to 100 outbox rows"
  w.n1: "SKIP LOCKED lets several workers\nrun without fighting over rows"
}

project: Project each ticket to a sheet row {
  w -> db: "JOIN tickets, employees,\nusers, lookups"
  db -> w: "names + labels"
  w.n2: "IDs resolved to display names HERE,\nat the boundary — never stored\ndenormalized in Postgres"
}

auth: Authenticate to Google {
  w -> sa: "sign JWT with service account key"
  sa -> gs: "exchange for access token"
  gs -> sa: "access token (1h)"
  sa -> w: "token, cached until expiry"
}

write: {
  new_ticket: {
    w -> gs: "values.append → _raw"
    gs -> raw: "row appended at the bottom"
    raw -> gs: "updatedRange = _raw!A57"
    gs -> w: "row 57"
    w -> db: "UPDATE outbox SET status='SENT',\nraw_row_number = 57, sent_at = now()"
  }
  existing_row_cached: {
    w -> gs: "values.update at _raw!A{raw_row_number}"
    w.n3: "Safe because _raw is append-only —\nrow 57 is row 57 forever"
    gs -> raw: "row updated in place"
    gs -> w: "200 OK"
    w -> db: "UPDATE outbox SET status='SENT'"
  }
  existing_row_not_cached: {
    w -> gs: "values.get _raw!B:B"
    gs -> w: "ticket_no column"
    w.n4: "locate row_key (ticket_no) by CONTENT,\nnever by remembered position"
    w -> gs: "values.update at the located row"
    w -> db: "UPDATE outbox SET status='SENT',\nraw_row_number = N"
  }
}

raw -> view: "QUERY recalculates —\nnewest-first, automatically"

failure: {
  transient: {
    gs -> w: "429 rate limited / 5xx"
    w -> db: "UPDATE outbox SET attempts = attempts + 1,\nlast_error = :error"
    w -> q: "requeue with exponential backoff"
    w.n5: "Encoding is unaffected.\nThe user never sees this."
  }
  permanent: {
    gs -> w: "403 / 400 after 5 attempts"
    w -> db: "UPDATE outbox SET status='FAILED'"
    w -> ops: "alert — dead letter"
  }
}

note: |md
  **What SyncOutbox is**

  A to-do list for Google. One row per ticket whose sheet entry is out of date,
  written in the same transaction as the ticket itself, so "the ticket exists" and
  "the sheet owes an update" become true together. The worker drains it; `status`
  moves PENDING → SENT, or → FAILED after five attempts.

  It lives in Postgres rather than only in Redis for that reason: a Redis dispatch
  can be lost while the ticket commits, and nothing would ever know the sheet was
  stale. The Redis job is only a nudge — the outbox is the record.

  **Why this runs out of band**

  Google Sheets is slow, rate limited (~60 writes/min/user), and owned by someone
  else. Inside the encoding request, every ticket save would inherit Google's latency
  and every Google outage would look like the system being down. The outbox buys
  separation: the ticket is safe the moment it commits, and the sheet catches up.

  **Batching matters most at backfill.** The existing sheet history goes through this
  same path. One batched call per 100 rows stays under the rate limit; 100 individual
  calls will not.

  **`raw_row_number` is only ever a cache.** If it is missing or stale, the worker
  falls back to locating the row by `row_key`. Correctness never depends on the
  cached index — that was the original bug.
| {near: bottom-center; shape: text}
